{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And you shall be to us for eyes.</b> To show us the way. And one may ask: since Israel traveled by the cloud and it guided them on the way, what need did they have for Jethro? But Moses said this in order to strengthen the heart of those of little faith among them, whose hearts were more comforted and strengthened by a leader of flesh and blood. <b>Or one may say:</b> 'And you shall be to us for eyes' — as testimony before the nations of what you saw with your own eyes, the great signs and wonders, so that they may see, take instruction, and come under the wings of the Shekhinah. And this is the view of Onkelos, of blessed memory, who translated: וגבורן דאתעבידא לנא חזיתא בעיניך — 'and the mighty deeds that were done for us you saw with your eyes.'" "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> 'And you shall be for us for eyes' — anything that is hidden from our eyes, may you illuminate our eyes. Another interpretation: may you be as dear to us as the apple of our eyes, as it is said (Deuteronomy 10:19): ״ואהבתם את הגר״ — 'and you shall love the stranger.'"], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:31:03.890765709Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}